        <fig id="article-19454.image.f1" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Resected Choledochal Cyst Specimen.&#x000a0;This surgical specimen displays the complete removal of a cystic dilation of the bile duct. The image shows the characteristic bulbous mass of the cyst alongside the attached ductal structures following successful resection. Contributed by Sunil Munakomi, MD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="CHOLEDOCHAL__CYST" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-19454.s17">
        <fig id="article-19454.image.f2" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Todani Classification of Choledochal Cysts. This illustration&#x000a0;shows the&#x000a0;5 types of choledochal cysts according to the Todani classification. Type I involves cystic or fusiform dilatation of the extrahepatic bile duct. Type II represents a true diverticulum arising from the common bile duct. Type III, or choledochocele, is defined as cystic dilatation of the distal common bile duct within the duodenum. Type IV includes multiple cysts affecting both the extrahepatic and intrahepatic bile ducts. Type V, also known as Caroli disease, is characterized by cystic dilatation confined to the intrahepatic bile ducts. Attribution: Adapted from Drriad. Wiki <ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/deed.en">Creative Commons 3.0 license</ext-link>.</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="t__x4x" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
